course: dictionary
lesson: 14100
title: A menagerie of meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A menagerie is a collection of wild or unusual animals kept so that people can come and see them. Long ago, kings kept menageries, such as the Royal Menagerie at the Tower of London, which had lions and a polar bear.
explain: Look at the clues in «The old king kept a menagerie of lions and bears.» The word kept and the animals lions and bears tell you a menagerie is a collection of animals. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The old king kept a collection of wild animals" still makes sense, so you can trust it.
explain: The word can also be informal, meaning a mixed group of many different things or people. «Our bedroom is a menagerie of toys, socks and books» has no animals in it at all. It means a jumble of many different things, so the words around it tell you which meaning fits.
fact: A menagerie is a collection of wild or unusual animals kept to be shown to people.
fact: Menageries were common in the past, and they were an early kind of zoo.
fact: The Royal Menagerie at the Tower of London had lions and a polar bear.
fact: Today, zoos also focus on caring for animals and saving species.
fact: Informally, «a menagerie» can mean any mixed group of different animals, or of strange and different people or things.
exercise: «The old king kept a menagerie of lions and bears.» What is a menagerie in this sentence? | A royal crown with jewels | *A collection of wild animals kept to be shown | A school for young artists | A garden full of flowers
exercise: «Our bedroom is a menagerie of toys, socks and books.» What does menagerie mean here? | A zoo for lions and bears | A quiet room for sleeping | *A messy, mixed group of many different things | A place where animals are kept safe
exercise: Find and copy the word in «The Royal Menagerie at the Tower of London had lions and a polar bear.» that names a place where animals were once shown. | Polar | London | Royal | *Menagerie
test: «Our family is a menagerie: two dogs, three cats, a rabbit and a noisy parrot.» What does menagerie suggest? | A family that hates animals | A family with only one pet | A family who lives in a castle | *A family with lots of different pets
test: In history, kings kept menageries. What was a menagerie in the past? | A shop that sold pets | A school for training horses | *A kind of early zoo, where wild or unusual animals were shown to people | A kind of early zoo, where wild or unusual animals were shown
exam: Mia says, «The toy box is a menagerie of plastic dinosaurs, rubber frogs and tin soldiers.» Which meaning fits, and why? | A quiet room, because toy boxes are always quiet | Animals in a zoo, because dinosaurs are animals | A royal palace, because kings kept menageries | *A mixed group of different things, because the words around it describe a jumble of toys
